item.rtg.fact.0=One gram of Pu-238 costs $8,000.
item.rtg.fact.1=One gram of Pu-238 produces just under half a Watt of decay heat.
item.rtg.fact.10=Natural uranium consists of 99.284% U-238, 0.711% U-235, and 0.0055% U-234.
item.rtg.fact.11=Most nuclear reactors use uranium that has been enriched to 3-5% U-235.
item.rtg.fact.12=Uranium-based nuclear weapons require uranium enriched to at least 85-90% U-235.
item.rtg.fact.13=Depleted uranium is uranium that has had most of its U-235 removed. It is effectively pure U-238.
item.rtg.fact.14=In the 1920s, uranium was considered a useless byproduct of the production of radium.
item.rtg.fact.15=The Manhattan Project referred to refined natural uranium as tuballoy, enriched uranium as oralloy, and depleted uranium as depletalloy.
item.rtg.fact.2=The typical plutonium RTG contains close to eight kilograms of Pu-238.
item.rtg.fact.3=Pu-238's half life is 87.7 years.
item.rtg.fact.4=A plutonium RTG was used to power the New Horizons probe that visited Pluto in 2015.
item.rtg.fact.5=Po-210 can also be used for RTGs as it generates 140 W/g of decay heat due to its 138 day half life.
item.rtg.fact.6=Pa-231 is an isotope of protactinium that easily fissions, but it isn't quite fissile.
item.rtg.fact.7=Muons generated by matter-antimatter reactions can trigger nuclear fusion reactions at room temperature.
item.rtg.fact.8=Roughly 20% of U-235 nuclei will fail to fission when hit by a neutron. They become U-236 nuclei instead.
item.rtg.fact.9=Thorium reactors are really uranium reactors that convert thorium into U-233.
